0; UofR and the largest gift in ¶16; Kodak Park in ¶9","shared_from":null,"also":true,"stood_on_para":null,"top":true,"stood_on":"--- ## George Eastman: the argument"},{"id":"c172","text":"The other men with fortunes that size bought immortality with them. Carnegie scattered his name across twenty-five hundred libraries in fourteen hundred towns. Rockefeller endowed a foundation that is still spending his money a century after his death. Eastman built no foundation, endowed no perpetuity, and took Rochester. He was betting that one place done completely was worth more than every place done a little, and the bet was never sentimental. Kodak ran on chemists, physicists, lens grinders, and managers, and it had to draw them to a mid-sized city on the Genesee and then keep them there against every offer from New York and Boston. So the schools he funded trained his technicians, the university supplied his laboratories, and the hospitals, the parks, and the orchestra made Rochester a city that punched far above its weight. Rochester was as much an operation he ran as Kodak, and it is one more thing he built that others got the credit for: every company that has since put a campus where its people would want to live is running his play. \n\nHe gave the way he manufactured: directly, in volume, while alive, into things that operate. When he died there was no machinery left running, because the machinery was never the point. The point was the city, and the city still collects. His gifts have outlived the company that paid for them: the university and the hospital he endowed are today Rochester's largest employer. That job used to belong to Kodak.","written_state":365,"written_at":"2026-08-20T11:44","resolved_state":452,"resolved_at":"2026-08-20T12:48","minutes_open":64.2,"resolved_by":"machine","basis":"position","confidence":"high","why":"Drafted block is p14-p16 merged; opens with p14's text","shared_from":null,"also":false,"stood_on_para":null,"top":false,"stood_on":"%%Th